        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Products</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these products. The picks were made from manufacturer specifications
            and manuals, published hands-on reviews, retailer listings, warranty terms and the US
            Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database, all checked on 16 October 2026.
            The Sole F63 has
            <a href="/sole-f63-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">its own review on this site</a>,
            and that review is also a reading of the published evidence, not a hands-on test.
          </p>
          <p>
            Four rules decided the list. <strong>Currently sold:</strong> each product had to be on
            sale on its maker's own store, under that name, on the day we checked.
            <strong>Agreement:</strong> the core figures — capacity, size, range of adjustment —
            had to match between the maker and a source that does not sell the product.
            <strong>A reachable maker:</strong> every brand here sells direct and publishes its
            warranty, so there is someone to call when a part fails after the retailer's return
            window. <strong>No safety action:</strong> we searched the CPSC's recall database by
            brand name for every maker on the list, and checked its published warnings.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What we left out, and why</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>BowFlex SelectTech 552 and 1090 dumbbells.</strong> They are the best-known
            adjustable dumbbells in the US, and in June 2025 the CPSC announced a recall of both
            models because the weight plates can dislodge from the handle during use. The recall
            notice tells owners to stop using them. Until the remedy is complete across the units
            still in circulation, we are not recommending either. Separately, recalls have also
            covered the Amazon Basics 55 lb adjustable dumbbells, two FitRx SmartBell models, and
            NordicTrack and ProForm 50 lb adjustable dumbbells, so if you are buying any adjustable
            dumbbell secondhand, check the model against the CPSC database first.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>NordicTrack RW900 rower.</strong> In October 2025 iFIT recalled NordicTrack
            rowing machines with model number NTRW19147 because the screen console can overheat and
            ignite. The remedy is a repair, but a recalled machine is not one we would point a new
            buyer towards.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sperax walking pads.</strong> The CPSC has warned consumers to stop using Sperax
            walking pads and treadmills immediately, and the seller has not agreed to a recall.
            They are excluded from every list on this site.
          </p>
          <p>
            We also passed over several strong products because their figures could not be settled
            on the day — a common problem after a model refresh, when the maker's page has moved on
            and the reviews have not — and over brands that sell only through marketplaces with no
            published warranty terms we could read.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="equipment-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The Equipment Types Explained</h2>
<p>
            "Fitness gym equipment" covers a lot of hardware, but a home gym is usually built from
            seven types. Here is what each one trains, how much room it takes and what it typically
            costs. The cost bands are drawn from the prices we saw for the products on this page and
            typical new prices from established brands; treat them as a rough guide to the market, not a promise.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Treadmill</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Trains:</strong> walking, running and incline walking — the most natural
            movement there is, and the easiest to start with. <strong>Space:</strong> the largest
            footprint of any cardio machine. A full-size folding treadmill such as the BowFlex T6 is
            76 by 35 inches set up, and you need clear space behind it. <strong>Cost band:</strong>
            walking pads from roughly $150 to $400; full-size folding treadmills with a 60-inch deck
            from roughly $900 to $1,500 on sale. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for home</a>
            roundup is the deeper read.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Exercise bike</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Trains:</strong> legs and the cardiovascular system, seated, with no impact.
            Indoor cycling bikes like the Schwinn IC4 suit structured classes; recumbent bikes suit
            anyone who wants back support. <strong>Space:</strong> modest — the IC4 is about 55 by
            31 inches. <strong>Cost band:</strong> roughly $300 to $1,000 for a bike with magnetic
            resistance, more for one with a built-in screen.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Rowing machine</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Trains:</strong> legs, back and arms in one movement, seated and low-impact. It
            rewards technique more than any other cardio machine. <strong>Space:</strong> long and
            narrow — the Concept2 is 96 by 24 inches — but most rowers stand on end or split for
            storage. <strong>Cost band:</strong> roughly $300 to $1,200, with the best-known air
            rowers near the top.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Elliptical</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Trains:</strong> legs, with moving handles adding arms, in a gliding motion
            where your feet never leave the pedals. <strong>Space:</strong> a footprint similar to a
            bike but much taller, because you stand on pedals that rise as they travel; check the
            ceiling. <strong>Cost band:</strong> roughly $500 to $2,500. See our
            <a href="/best-elliptical-machines-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best elliptical machines for home</a>.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Adjustable dumbbells</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Trains:</strong> almost everything — presses, rows, squats, lunges, curls — with
            one pair replacing a rack of fixed weights. <strong>Space:</strong> a stand about the
            size of a small side table. <strong>Cost band:</strong> roughly $200 to $900 for a pair,
            depending on maximum weight and build. Our
            <a href="/best-adjustable-dumbbells/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best adjustable dumbbells</a>
            page compares the mechanisms.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Squat or power rack</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Trains:</strong> heavy barbell squats, bench presses and overhead presses, with
            safeties that catch the bar if you fail a rep — which is what makes heavy lifting alone
            reasonably safe. <strong>Space:</strong> about 4 by 5 feet of floor and, for most
            full-height racks, at least 7 feet of ceiling; the REP PR-1100 is 85 inches tall.
            <strong>Cost band:</strong> roughly $300 to $1,500, before a barbell and plates. The
            <a href="/best-squat-racks-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best squat racks for a home gym</a>
            guide goes through heights, depths and attachments.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Adjustable bench</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Trains:</strong> nothing on its own, but it turns dumbbells and a rack into a
            complete upper-body setup: flat, incline and, on some benches, decline pressing and
            rowing. <strong>Space:</strong> about 2 by 5 feet, and it slides under a rack or against
            a wall. <strong>Cost band:</strong> roughly $150 to $600.
          </p>
          <p>
            Beyond these seven, a home gym tends to grow into cable machines, plate-loaded
            machines, platforms, accessories and recovery tools. Each has its own page, listed in
            the final section below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reading-reviews" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. How to Read Exercise Equipment Reviews Honestly</h2>
<p>
            The same habits that protect you when buying a treadmill work for every category; our
            guide on
            <a href="/how-to-read-treadmill-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to read treadmill reviews</a>
            goes into them in detail. The short version is to ask three questions of any review
            before you believe its verdict: who paid for it, what was actually done, and do its
            numbers match the maker's.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Sponsored, affiliate and independent</h3>
          <p>
            Most equipment reviews online earn money somehow, this one included. That is not a
            problem in itself; what matters is whether the money changes the verdict. There are
            three common arrangements. An <strong>affiliate review</strong> earns a commission when
            you buy through its links, and should say so near the top. A <strong>sponsored
            review</strong> is paid for or supplied by the maker, and in the US the reviewer is
            expected to disclose that clearly; a review that thanks a brand for "sending this over"
            in the last paragraph is sponsored, whatever it calls itself. An <strong>independent
            review</strong> bought the product with its own money. Independent reviews are rare and
            valuable, but buying does not by itself make a reviewer careful.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What a real test should include</h3>
          <p>
            If a review claims to have tested a product, it should tell you how. A useful hands-on
            review of a machine names who used it, for how long, and at what settings; describes
            assembly and how long it took; reports what was measured and with what (a decibel
            figure needs a distance and a meter); shows the product in the reviewer's own space
            rather than in press photographs; and says what went wrong. A strength review should
            say what weights were used and whether the reviewer loaded the product anywhere near its
            rated capacity, because almost nobody does. A review that cannot tell you any of this
            has described the product, not tested it — which is fine, as long as it does not
            pretend otherwise. This page describes; it does not test.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Red flags</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Fake urgency.</strong> Countdown timers, "only 3 left" banners and
            permanent "today only" sales are there to stop you comparing. Good equipment is still
            for sale next week.</li>
            <li><strong>Ratings without a method.</strong> A 9.4 out of 10 means nothing unless the
            review says what was scored and how. If every product on a list scores between 9 and
            10, the scale is decoration.</li>
            <li><strong>Figures that conflict between listings.</strong> If the maker says 350 lb
            and a review says 375 lb, one of them is describing a different version, or is wrong.
            Model refreshes are the usual cause — we found exactly this on two of the products on
            this page. Trust the maker's current page and the manual for the unit you are buying.</li>
            <li><strong>Only positives.</strong> Every product has a weakness. A review that cannot
            name one has not looked.</li>
            <li><strong>Peak figures presented as capability.</strong> Peak horsepower on a
            treadmill, "supports 1,000 lb" on a cheap rack with no test standard, "replaces 28
            dumbbells" — these are marketing, not specification.</li>
            <li><strong>No date.</strong> Prices, models and warranty terms change constantly. A
            review with no checked-on date may be describing last year's machine.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="where-to-find" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Where to Find Exercise Equipment Reviews Worth Trusting</h2>
<p>
            No single source is enough. The approach that works is to triangulate: read what the
            maker claims, see whether an independent source agrees, and check what owners say after
            a year rather than a week.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>The maker's own product page and manual.</strong> Not for opinions, but for
            the current specification, the warranty terms and the space and ceiling requirements.
            The manual is often more honest than the product page: it is where you find the
            maximum user weight the warranty actually honours and the clearance the maker expects
            behind a treadmill.</li>
            <li><strong>Hands-on reviewers who publish their method.</strong> The better review
            sites explain how they score equipment and show it in use. Read their cons more
            carefully than their pros.</li>
            <li><strong>Owner reviews, sorted by most recent and lowest rating.</strong> The
            one-star reviews tell you how a company handles a failure, which is what you are really
            buying with a warranty. Look for patterns — the same part failing at the same point —
            rather than single angry posts.</li>
            <li><strong>The CPSC recall database.</strong> Search by brand and by product type
            before you buy anything with a motor, a moving stack or a load rating, and before you
            buy anything secondhand.</li>
            <li><strong>Forums and communities for the sport.</strong> Rowers, lifters and runners
            talk about equipment for years after the launch reviews stop. They are the best source
            on long-term durability.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            If you are deciding between equipment at home and a membership, run the numbers in our
            <a href="/home-treadmill-vs-gym-membership/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home treadmill vs gym membership</a>
            comparison first. The same logic applies to a whole home gym, and for some people the
            honest answer is the membership.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-check" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. How This Site Checks Products: Behind the Scenes</h2>
<p>
            Since this is a page about honest reviewing, here is exactly how ours are made, including
            what we do not do.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>We do not test.</strong> Our product pages and roundups are built from published
            evidence, and we say so at the top of every one. You will never read a claim on this site
            that we tested something, because it would not be true. Where we pass on what someone found using a
            product, we say who: "a hands-on reviewer reported", "owners commonly report".
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>We cross-check every figure.</strong> For each product we read the maker's
            current page and, where available, the manual, then compare the figures against at
            least one source that does not sell the product — a published review or a second
            retailer. A figure goes on the card only if they agree. When they do not, we leave it out
            and say so, because printing one of two conflicting numbers is a guess dressed up as a
            fact. On this page that meant dropping unit weights for three products and adjustment
            counts for the bench.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>We check safety records.</strong> Every brand on a list is searched in the CPSC
            recall database and its published warnings on the day we check. A product under a
            recall or a stop-using warning does not appear as a pick, and we name the exclusion so
            you know it was considered.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>We date everything.</strong> Every roundup states the day it was checked.
            Prices are what we saw that day, written as a range, and we do not quote subscription
            prices for fitness apps because they change too often to be useful.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>We say how we are paid.</strong> We earn a commission on purchases through our
            Amazon links. That is the business, and we disclose it in the note at the top of every
            roundup. It does not decide which products appear: several picks on this page are ones we
            confirmed only on their makers' own stores, where we earn nothing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="strength-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Using Strength Equipment Safely at Home</h2>
<p>
            Cardio machines have safety keys and stop buttons. Strength equipment relies on you, so
            it is worth being plain about the basics. This is general guidance about equipment, not
            coaching.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Rated capacities are manufacturer figures.</strong> A rack "rated to 700
            lb" or a bench "rated to 1,000 lb" is the maker's statement, usually including your body
            weight on a bench, and there is no single test standard behind those numbers across
            brands. Treat them as ceilings, not targets, and stay well inside them.</li>
            <li><strong>Anchor or bolt racks exactly as the maker specifies.</strong> Some racks are
            designed to be bolted to the floor or a wall; some, like the REP PR-1100, cannot be bolted
            down at all, and REP advises against dynamic movements such as kipping or muscle-ups on
            its pull-up bar. Read the manual before you decide how to use it.</li>
            <li><strong>Use the safeties every time.</strong> Set the safety bars or spotter arms
            just below the bottom of your range of motion on squats and bench presses. They are the
            reason a rack makes heavy lifting alone reasonably safe; a rack used without them is an
            expensive bar holder.</li>
            <li><strong>Check floor loading for heavy racks upstairs.</strong> A loaded rack,
            barbell and plates concentrate a lot of weight on a small footprint, and dropping
            weights adds shock loads. Ground floors and garages on concrete are the easy case. For
            an upper floor, a converted loft or anything unusual about the structure, consult a
            structural engineer or builder before installing heavy equipment. Our
            <a href="/best-lifting-platforms/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best lifting platforms</a>
            guide covers protecting the floor itself.</li>
            <li><strong>Inspect adjustable dumbbells before each session.</strong> Check that the
            selector pin or dial is fully engaged and that nothing rattles. The BowFlex recall described
            above involved plates dislodging from the handle during use.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep children away from equipment.</strong> Store dumbbells on their stand,
            and keep plates off the floor where they can be pulled or tipped.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="budget-order" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Buying Order for a Home Gym on a Budget</h2>
<p>
            Few people buy all seven pieces at once, and they should not have to. The order below
            gets the most training out of each dollar, assuming you want both cardio and strength.
            Change it to match your priorities, not ours.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Adjustable dumbbells.</strong> The most versatile thing you can buy for a
            few hundred dollars, and they fit in any room. A 5 to 50 lb pair covers most people for
            a long time.</li>
            <li><strong>An adjustable bench.</strong> It roughly doubles what the dumbbells can do,
            for a fraction of their price.</li>
            <li><strong>One cardio machine — the one you will use.</strong> Not the one with the
            best reviews. If you like walking, a treadmill or walking pad; if your knees complain, a
            bike or elliptical; if you want the most work in the least time and are willing to learn
            technique, a rower. The
            <a href="/best-cardio-machines-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best cardio machines for home</a>
            roundup compares every type.</li>
            <li><strong>Flooring.</strong> Rubber mats protect the floor and the equipment and cut
            noise. It is the purchase people most regret skipping. See
            <a href="/best-home-gym-accessories/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home gym accessories</a>
            and, for treadmills,
            <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill mats</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>A rack, barbell and plates.</strong> The big step: it is where strength
            training gets serious, and where space and floor loading start to matter. Budget for
            the barbell and plates as well as the rack; they often cost more than it does.</li>
            <li><strong>Everything else.</strong> Cable machines, specialist machines,
            <a href="/best-home-gym-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">all-in-one home gym machines</a>,
            recovery tools — once you know from experience what is missing.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Two money-saving notes. Strength equipment holds its value and has few parts to wear,
            so a secondhand rack, bench or set of plates is usually a safer bet than a secondhand
            treadmill. And watch for sales: on three of the four cardio picks, the price we saw was
            $100 to $200 below list when we checked.
          </p>
        </div>

