        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best home workout equipment to buy first?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, a pair of adjustable dumbbells. They train almost every muscle group, fit in a small room and cost a few hundred dollars. Add an adjustable bench next, then one cardio machine that matches how you like to exercise. A rack, barbell and plates come later, once you know you will use them and have checked your space and floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where can I find honest exercise equipment reviews?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Combine sources rather than trusting one. Read the maker's page and manual for specifications and warranty, a hands-on reviewer who explains their method for performance, and recent low-rated owner reviews for how the company handles failures. Check the CPSC recall database for safety. Treat any review with no date, no method and no negatives with suspicion.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How can you tell if an equipment review is sponsored?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look for a disclosure near the top that mentions affiliate links, free products or payment from the brand. In the US, reviewers are expected to disclose such relationships clearly. Phrases like "thanks to the brand for sending this" buried at the end mean the review is sponsored. A review that only lists positives, or uses urgency to push a sale, deserves caution either way.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the main types of home gym equipment?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most home gyms are built from seven types: treadmills, exercise bikes, rowing machines and ellipticals for cardio, and adjustable dumbbells, a squat or power rack, and an adjustable bench for strength. Beyond those come cable machines, plate-loaded machines, lifting platforms, accessories such as flooring and storage, and recovery tools.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do specifications differ between equipment listings?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually because a product was updated without a new name, and older reviews or retailer listings still describe the previous version. Retailers also copy specifications loosely between models. When figures disagree, trust the maker's current product page and the manual for the unit you are buying, and confirm anything important, such as capacity or dimensions, with the seller in writing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to put a squat rack in an upstairs room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be, but check first. A loaded rack concentrates a lot of weight on a small area, and dropped weights add shock loads. Ground floors and concrete garage slabs are straightforward. For an upper floor, a loft or any unusual structure, ask a structural engineer or builder before installing, use proper flooring, and follow the rack maker's instructions on anchoring.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Buying Home Workout Equipment</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most regrets in a home gym come from buying in the wrong order or believing the wrong number, not from choosing the wrong brand.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the machine with the best reviews instead of the one you will use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A top-rated rower is a poor buy for someone who hates rowing. Equipment only works if it gets used, and enjoyment predicts use better than any specification. If you are unsure, try the movement at a gym or a friend’s house first, or start with the cheapest version of the category before committing to the expensive one.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a capacity figure without checking where it came from</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A rack or bench rating is the maker’s own number, and an old review may quote a figure from an earlier version. Check the current spec sheet, stay well inside the rating, and remember that a bench capacity usually includes your body weight. For racks, read how the maker expects it to be anchored before you load it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the space around the equipment</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A footprint is not the space you need. Treadmills need clearance behind, ellipticals and racks need ceiling height, rowers need room to sit and move, and barbell work needs room at both ends of the bar. Measure the room with the equipment in use, not just standing still, before you order.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying secondhand without checking for recalls</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Several adjustable dumbbells, rowers and treadmills sold in recent years have been recalled, and a secondhand seller may not know. Before you buy anything used, find the model and serial number and check them against the CPSC recall database. For powered machines, also ask for proof of purchase so the warranty question is settled.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

